{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"73651a459f29dd7450c6c7cc39df246c43793a49"},"timestamp":1535432893,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNjQ1Mjg1Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2783#issuecomment-416452857"},"message":"[GitMate.io](https://gitmate.io) thinks possibly related issues are https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/704 ([wishlist] publish --recursive=changed), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2022 (How shall we revert master?), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/749 ([wishlist] foreach command), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2473 (Should `run` have --to-git?), and https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/445 (Decide on the nature of --recursive).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1535461460,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNjU3NTkwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2783#issuecomment-416575902"},"message":"\u003e it would be impossible to cleanly revert in the superdataset,\n\nI don't understand this point.\n\n\u003e in submodule commit difference might correspond to more than a single commit (Then I guess optionally we could try to revert full sequence of them)\n\nIt seems like what you're thinking of is some sort of \"revert the last commit(s) added by the a datalad command\".  Of course, we don't have any way of knowing (or perhaps even defining clearly) that a certain set of commits were added by the same command/action.\n\nWithout such information, I don't see how this command would work because there doesn't seem to be a way to spell out clearly what should be reverted.  If you gave a specific commit hexsha, that would work for only one dataset, so you'd be limited to giving relative identifiers like `HEAD~1`, `HEAD~3..` under the assumption that the line up across datasets.\n\nOn top of that, there's the difficulty of conflicts and supporting git-revert's sequencer behavior.\n\n[After further thought, @yarikoptic-gitmate wonders if this is related to #1424]","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1535461460,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEyMzk1MTIwMw=="},"target":"e5eaf5a93d1081bde36659985078c92f49623194f52458d5924be50aaaa9f42a","message":"\u003e it would be impossible to cleanly revert in the superdataset,\n\nI don't understand this point.\n\n\u003e in submodule commit difference might correspond to more than a single commit (Then I guess optionally we could try to revert full sequence of them)\n\nIt seems like what you're thinking of is some sort of \"revert the last commit(s) added by the a datalad command\".  Of course, we don't have any way of knowing (or perhaps even defining clearly) that a certain set of commits were added by the same command/action.\n\nWithout such information, I don't see how this command would work because there doesn't seem to be a way to spell out clearly what should be reverted.  If you gave a specific commit hexsha, that would work for only one dataset, so you'd be limited to giving relative identifiers like `HEAD~1`, `HEAD~3..` under the assumption that they line up across datasets.\n\nOn top of that, there's the difficulty of conflicts and supporting git-revert's sequencer behavior.\n\n[After further thought, @yarikoptic-gitmate wonders if this is related to #1424]","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1535466070,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNjYwMzQyMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2783#issuecomment-416603420"},"message":"\u003e revert the last commit(s) \n\nNot necessarily the last. It is literally just a wish for recursive operation to `git revert`.  Happens eg if I need to rerun some earlier conversion in heudiconv\n\n#1424 is close to checkout and/or reset --hard as far as I see it","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1535470268,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNjYzMTg0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2783#issuecomment-416631840"},"message":"\u003e Not necessarily the last. \n\nThat's fine.  My remaining comment addressed the general case.\n\n\u003e It is literally just a wish for recursive operation to git revert.\n\nTo rephrase my comment: I think that's an undefined operation.  DataLad would need to store a lot of complicated information to make it defined.\n\n\u003e #1424 is close to checkout and/or reset --hard as far as I see it\n\nYes, it is.  I think it's worth thinking about whether any information stored about the state for that purpose could help here.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537896541,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NjYzOTczOTc="},"added":["waiting-for-directmode-death"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1551638021,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50MjE3NjQzMzEyNQ=="},"added":[],"removed":["waiting-for-directmode-death"]}]}